  "samplingHelpIntro": "Temperature and Top-P control how Maia chooses between moves.",
  "@samplingHelpIntro": {
    "description": "User-approved Maia-specific sampling help, paragraph 1. Preserve the strength/variety distinction, the direct-match result at setting 1600, and probability mass (not number of moves). Use established local chess terminology."
  },
  "samplingHelpVariety": "Increasing Temperature and Top-P is like making Maia a more volatile player, as Maia becomes more likely to play low-probability moves. These may be blunders, but they may also be brilliant moves!",
  "@samplingHelpVariety": {
    "description": "User-approved Maia-specific sampling help, paragraph 2. Preserve the strength/variety distinction, the direct-match result at setting 1600, and probability mass (not number of moves). Use established local chess terminology."
  },
  "samplingHelpDefaults": "If you value maximum variety, and you also want Maia to play a broad range of human openings, including sidelines, then keep the default values of 1.0 and 1.0.",
  "@samplingHelpDefaults": {
    "description": "User-approved Maia-specific sampling help, paragraph 3. Preserve the strength/variety distinction, the direct-match result at setting 1600, and probability mass (not number of moves). Use established local chess terminology."
  },
  "samplingHelpStrength": "If you prefer a stronger opponent playing fewer blunders, experiment with moving Temperature and Top-P values down.",
  "@samplingHelpStrength": {
    "description": "User-approved Maia-specific sampling help, paragraph 4. Preserve the strength/variety distinction, the direct-match result at setting 1600, and probability mass (not number of moves). Use established local chess terminology."
  },
  "samplingHelpArgmax": "Setting Temperature to 0 will cause Maia to use argmax, which is a technical way of saying that Maia will always play what it predicts is the most probable human move in a given position. Careful! In our research at the 1600 setting, argmax Maia outperformed Maia at 1.0 / 1.0 by the equivalent of about 300 Elo points in direct matches.",
  "@samplingHelpArgmax": {
    "description": "User-approved Maia-specific sampling help, paragraph 5. Preserve the strength/variety distinction, the direct-match result at setting 1600, and probability mass (not number of moves). Use established local chess terminology."
  },
  "samplingHelpTopP": "For balanced play, you may consider keeping Temperature at 1.0 and setting Top-P to 0.95. This maintains variety while eliminating low-probability moves, which can often be blunders—but can also be good moves. Top-P essentially cuts off low-probability moves. Setting it to 0.95 keeps moves accounting for at least 95% of the probability after Temperature is applied. The remaining moves cannot be selected by Maia.",
  "@samplingHelpTopP": {
    "description": "User-approved Maia-specific sampling help, paragraph 6. Preserve the strength/variety distinction, the direct-match result at setting 1600, and probability mass (not number of moves). Use established local chess terminology."
  },
  "samplingHelpResearchIntro": "We experimented with these settings at 1600 Elo and published our full findings here:",
  "@samplingHelpResearchIntro": {
    "description": "User-approved Maia-specific sampling help, paragraph 7. Preserve the strength/variety distinction, the direct-match result at setting 1600, and probability mass (not number of moves). Use established local chess terminology."
  }
